dialogue cohesion_redmage_engineer
title: A gauge for the coal
trigger: Cohesion redmage engineer level=2
once
speaker commander $commander
speaker redmage redmage
speaker engineer engineer

node open commander
  The Engineer is lying on one elbow a careful arm's length from the Red Mage's tin, holding a brass gauge on a chain and not quite pointing it at the coal.
  -> nine

node nine engineer
  Nine years, you said. A coal doesn't last nine nights, not without feeding. You feed it peat, I've watched. That doesn't add up. Something in there is wrong in a way I'd like to understand.
  -> wrong

node wrong redmage
  Nothing in there is wrong. It's my aunt's hearth. Put the brass away before I melt it into a ring for you.
  -> warm

node warm engineer
  I'm not trying to take it. Halcyon seals run warm for a hundred years on nothing. If your coal does the same, that's a question with an answer. Commander, tell the Red Mage I'm harmless.
  * Let the Engineer measure it. Gauge only. The lid stays where the Red Mage puts it. -> measure
    do: AddCohesion redmage engineer 10
    do: AddTrust redmage -3
    do: SetFlag redmage_engineer:coal_measured
  * Ask the Red Mage, not the tin. The coal answers to one person. -> ask
    do: AddCohesion redmage engineer 6
    do: SetFlag redmage_engineer:coal_asked
  * A fire near the wagons is everyone's business. Measure it, Engineer. -> business
    do: AddCohesion redmage engineer -4
    do: AddTrust redmage -2
    do: SetFlag redmage_engineer:coal_business
  * I let the Engineer open the orb, and nothing broke. A gauge on a tin is less than that. -> orb
    if: FlagSet engineer:orb_lent
    do: AddCohesion redmage engineer 8
    do: AddTrust engineer 2
    do: SetFlag redmage_engineer:coal_orb

node measure redmage
  Gauge only. Fine. If that needle so much as twitches toward the lid, Engineer, I'm keeping it. Well? What does it say?
  -> seal

node ask engineer
  Ask. All right. Red Mage, may I? Please? I've a whole page for it. Page sixty. It's been empty since the fen.
  -> seal

node business redmage
  Everyone's business. It's been my business alone for nine years and the wagons are still here. Measure, then. I'll be over there, not watching.
  -> seal

node orb redmage
  The orb. And it's still glowing, I suppose. Fine. One look. One. My aunt would have liked you, Engineer. She liked people who asked first.
  -> seal

node seal engineer
  It reads like a seal. Old warmth, not fire warmth. Tap Seven ran like this until it didn't, and Redline put that on my account. If your coal is the same thing, it's worth more than the fish market.
  * Redline gets nothing, and the coal isn't for sale. Say it together and mean it. -> nothing
    if: FlagSet engineer:debt_defied
    do: AddCohesion redmage engineer 8
    do: SetFlag redmage_engineer:seal_nothing
  * The coal goes home to the aunt's hearth. Build it a grate that will hold it. -> grate
    if: FlagSet redmage:fire_home
    do: AddCohesion redmage engineer 10
    do: AddTrust redmage 2
    do: SetFlag redmage_engineer:seal_grate
  * Build the Red Mage a better tin. Vented, hinged, and never opened by anyone else. -> tin
    do: AddCohesion redmage engineer 8
    do: SetFlag redmage_engineer:seal_tin
  * Whatever it's worth, it stays a coal. No more questions about it. -> stays
    do: AddCohesion redmage engineer 2
    do: AddTrust engineer -2
    do: SetFlag redmage_engineer:seal_stays

node nothing redmage
  Nothing. Not one ember. If Redline asks, Engineer, tell them it went out years ago. I'll look very sad.
  -> close

node grate engineer
  A grate. Iron, not brass, brass goes soft. Draw me the hearth, Red Mage, and I'll have the fitting ready by the time we pass the fens.
  -> close

node tin engineer
  Double-walled. Vent in the lid, latch only you can work. I'll make the latch backwards. Nobody ever thinks to try it backwards.
  -> close

node stays engineer
  No more questions. Page sixty stays half full, then. That's harder for me than it sounds, Commander.
  -> close

node close commander
  The gauge goes back on its chain. The Red Mage turns the tin a little toward the Engineer, so the light from the dent falls on the open notebook.
